#  Wrapper Classes in Java



#  What are Wrapper Classes?

Wrapper classes are objects that wrap primitive data types into objects.

Java provides wrapper classes for all primitive types.



# Primitive vs Wrapper

| Primitive | Wrapper Class |
| --------- | ------------- |
| byte      | Byte          |
| short     | Short         |
| int       | Integer       |
| long      | Long          |
| float     | Float         |
| double    | Double        |
| char      | Character     |
| boolean   | Boolean       |


#  Why Wrapper Classes are Needed

Java collections store only objects.

Examples:

* ArrayList
* HashMap
* HashSet

Wrapper classes help convert primitives into objects.


# Example

int x = 10;

Integer obj = Integer.valueOf(x);


#  Boxing

Converting primitive → wrapper object



## Example
int num = 100;

Integer obj = Integer.valueOf(num);



# Unboxing

Converting wrapper object → primitive


##  Example
Integer obj = 200;

int num = obj.intValue();



# Autoboxing

Automatic primitive → wrapper conversion



##  Example

Integer obj = 50;


Compiler internally:

Integer.valueOf(50)




# Auto-unboxing

Automatic wrapper → primitive conversion


## Example

Integer obj = 70;

int num = obj;


Compiler internally:
obj.intValue()




#  Useful Wrapper Methods

| Method      | Purpose                    |
| ----------- | -------------------------- |
| parseInt()  | String → int               |
| valueOf()   | Primitive/String → Wrapper |
| toString()  | Convert to String          |
| compareTo() | Compare values             |



#  Example

String s = "100";

int x = Integer.parseInt(s);



#  Why Collections Need Wrapper Classes

ArrayList<int> ❌

Valid:
ArrayList<Integer> ✅




#  Null Support

Primitive:
int x = null; ❌

Wrapper:
Integer x = null; ✅



#  Important Points

* Wrapper classes are immutable
* Stored in heap memory
* Allow use of utility methods
* Needed in generics & collections


# Performance Note

Primitive types are faster than wrappers because wrappers create objects.



#  Best Practices

* Use primitives for performance
* Use wrappers for collections/APIs
* Avoid unnecessary boxing/unboxing


#  Real-world Usage

* Collections Framework
* Database APIs
* JSON processing
* Frameworks like Spring/Hibernate
